>>
endobj
553 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 488 0 R
/T (Structural gaps persist, even as girls continue to outperform.)
/E (Structural gaps persist, even as girls continue to outperform.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
554 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 488 0 R
/E (Resilience is not a substitute for support.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
555 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 488 0 R
/E (Girls in Washington graduate at higher rates than boys, show increasing school engagement, and are seeking mental health care at growing rates. Girls are outperforming despite structural gaps in healthcare access, STEM opportunity, and safety. Celebrating resilience without addressing the conditions that demand it risks obscuring the work still that still needs to be done.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
556 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 488 0 R
/E (Aggregated data hides girls who need support.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
557 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 488 0 R
/E (Averages do not communicate the full facts. When "girls" is treated as a single category, elevated rates of depression, dating violence, and suicidal ideation among subpopulations go unaddressed.  Disaggregated data is not a methodological preference; it is a matter of visibility, and visibility is a prerequisite for equitable policy.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
558 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 488 0 R
/E (Focus more on middle school.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
559 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 488 0 R
/E (Nearly 1 in 4 sixth-grade girls reported contemplating suicide in 2023, and dating violence begins escalating in 8th grade, yet most prevention efforts remain concentrated in high school. Resources should shift earlier, focusing on prevention before risk behaviors are established.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
